# Auto-mounts any USB storage partition the instant it's plugged in, using
# udisks2 (already present on the Jetson - see usb_video_key.py, which
# reads lsblk/udevadm but never mounts anything itself) instead of a raw
# `mount` call, so it gets the same safe defaults (uid/gid, an
# /media/<user>/<label> mountpoint, clean auto-unmount on removal) a
# desktop session's own automount would normally provide.
#
# Needed because this Jetson runs headless (SSH/PyCharm only, no GUI
# login) - udisks2's own automount trigger normally only fires for an
# active local desktop session, which never exists here, so without this
# rule nothing ever tells it to mount an inserted stick (confirmed on real
# hardware 2026-09-22 - a plugged-in stick showed up via lsblk with
# mounted_at=None and just sat there forever).
#
# Runs as root (udev's own RUN+= always does), which udisks2/polkit treats
# as always-authorized to mount regardless of session state - so this
# works the same whether anyone is logged into a desktop or not.
#
# systemd-run --no-block is needed rather than calling udisksctl directly:
# udev rules must return quickly, and udisksctl's D-Bus round-trip can be
# slow enough to hit udev's own rule-execution timeout otherwise.
#
# Mounted with -o uid=...,gid=... forcing ownership to the "daniel" user
# app.py actually runs as (real bug hit 2026-09-22: without this, a mount
# triggered by root - which this rule always runs as, since udev's own
# RUN+= always does - lands at /media/root/<label> owned by root, and
# usb_video_key.py's select() then fails to create folders on it with
# Permission denied, since app.py itself is never run as root - see
# .idea/deployment.xml / PyCharm's remote interpreter config). FAT-family
# filesystems (vfat/exfat, what a USB stick normally ships with) have no
# native Unix permission bits, so udisks2 honours this option directly
# rather than needing a chown after the fact. `id -u`/`id -g` look the
# uid/gid up fresh each time rather than hardcoding a number, so this
# keeps working even if "daniel"'s uid ever changes.
#
# Calls out to usb-video-key-mount.sh (same directory) rather than
# inlining the mount command here - see that script's own comments for
# why a separate file is much easier to debug than a one-liner buried
# inside a udev rule's own quoting rules.
#
# One-time install (as root, on the Jetson):
#   sudo cp system/99-usb-video-key-automount.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo cp system/usb-video-key-mount.sh /usr/local/bin/
#   sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/usb-video-key-mount.sh
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
# No reboot needed - takes effect on the next USB insert. A stick that's
# already mounted (including one already wrongly mounted under
# /media/root/ by an older copy of this same rule) needs a one-time
# unplug/replug to pick this up - reloading the rule alone doesn't touch
# an already-inserted device.
#
# To test the actual mount logic directly, without touching udev/USB at
# all: `sudo /usr/local/bin/usb-video-key-mount.sh sda1` (swap sda1 for
# whatever `lsblk` shows) - any real failure prints straight to the
# terminal.

ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="partition", ENV{ID_BUS}=="usb", ENV{ID_FS_USAGE}=="filesystem", RUN+="/usr/bin/systemd-run --no-block /usr/local/bin/usb-video-key-mount.sh %k"
